{
  "id": "a2-quadratics",
  "chapterNumber": "2",
  "title": {
    "en": "Quadratics &amp; Complex Numbers",
    "ar": null
  },
  "chapterTag": {
    "variant": "category",
    "className": "a2-tag",
    "badgeClassName": "a2-paper",
    "emoji": "📌",
    "category": "Quadratics &amp; Complex Numbers",
    "weightBadge": "A2",
    "topics": [
      "Completing the square",
      "Discriminant",
      "Complex roots \\(a\\pm bi\\)"
    ]
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Every quadratic can be solved — and when the discriminant is negative, the solutions live in the <strong>complex numbers</strong>. This chapter ties graphing, factoring and the imaginary unit together.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 2.1 — The imaginary unit",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Define \\( i=\\sqrt{-1} \\), so \\( i^{2}=-1 \\). A <strong>complex number</strong> has the form \\( a+bi \\) with \\( a,b \\) real; add and multiply just as with binomials, replacing \\( i^{2} \\) by \\( -1 \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quadratic formula",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x=\\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Discriminant",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\Delta=b^{2}-4ac \\) decides the root type",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Vertex",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Powers of i",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( i^{2}=-1,\\ i^{3}=-i,\\ i^{4}=1 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Describe the roots of \\( x^{2}+2x+5=0 \\).</p><p>Discriminant \\( \\Delta=2^{2}-4(1)(5)=-16&lt;0 \\), so there are no real roots — two complex conjugates. Solving:</p>\\[ x=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{-16}}{2}=-1\\pm 2i. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Discriminant &amp; the parabola's roots",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "a2QuadCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "The graph of x squared plus bx plus c, with real roots marked when the discriminant is non-negative",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "a2QuadDataBtn",
          "panelId": "a2QuadDataPanel",
          "descId": "a2QuadDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "a2QuadDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Sampled points on f(x) = x² + bx + c",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "a2QuadB",
            "label": { "en": "<b>b</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -8, "max": 8, "value": 2, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "a2QuadBval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Coefficient b", "ar": null }
          },
          {
            "id": "a2QuadC",
            "label": { "en": "<b>c</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -10, "max": 10, "value": 5, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "a2QuadCval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Coefficient c", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "a2QuadD",
            "label": { "en": "discriminant D", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "-16"
          },
          {
            "id": "a2QuadType",
            "label": { "en": "root type", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "No real roots (complex)"
          },
          {
            "id": "a2QuadRoots",
            "label": { "en": "roots", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "-1.00 ± 2.00i"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "Default matches Worked example 2.A exactly: b = 2, c = 5 gives D = −16, so the graph never touches the x-axis and the roots are the complex pair −1 ± 2i. Drag D up through 0 and the (real) roots appear on the x-axis right when D crosses zero — the discriminant deciding the root type, live.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Multiply \\( (2+i)(3-i) \\).</p>\\[ 6-2i+3i-i^{2}=6+i+1=7+i. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve \\(x^2-4x+13=0\\) and express roots in the form \\(a\\pm bi\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Discriminant \\(=16-52=-36\\). \\(x=\\dfrac{4\\pm\\sqrt{-36}}{2}=\\dfrac{4\\pm6i}{2}=2\\pm3i\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
